  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Stage IA right breast cancer after mastectomy, on adjuvant tamoxifen (C50.411)</content>. No evidence of recurrence. Dr. Lozano agrees that tamoxifen alone is right for her node-negative, low recurrence-score cancer, without ovarian suppression or chemotherapy. [S]<list><item>Continue tamoxifen 20 mg daily for at least five years; consider ten years at the five-year review.</item><item>History and examination here every three months for the first year, with oncology review twice a year.</item><item>Diagnostic mammogram of the left breast in September, one year after diagnosis; no routine blood tests or body imaging.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Tamoxifen side effects: mild hot flashes (Z79.810)</content>. Mild vasomotor symptoms common in the first year. She prefers non-drug measures first. [S]<list><item>Layered clothing, a bedroom fan, cool drinks, and paced breathing; cognitive behavioral strategies handout given.</item><item>If they disturb sleep more often, venlafaxine is the first choice; avoid paroxetine, fluoxetine, and bupropion, which block the activation of tamoxifen.</item><item>Report any bleeding between periods, heavy bleeding, or leg swelling at once.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Absence of the right breast, by her choice without reconstruction or prosthesis (Z90.11)</content>. Healed. Her decision is recorded and respected. [S]<list><item>No further discussion unless she raises it.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Cancer-related history and physical examination</td><td>Every 3 to 6 months for the first 3 years</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No symptoms or signs of recurrence; chest wall and left breast normal</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Tamoxifen adherence and side effects</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Taken daily for five months; no missed doses</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Vasomotor symptom assessment</td><td>Periodically on endocrine therapy</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Three or four hot flashes a day; non-drug measures started</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Abnormal vaginal bleeding inquiry on tamoxifen</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No bleeding between periods; yearly gynecologic assessment due at the autumn preventive visit</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Venous thromboembolism symptom inquiry on tamoxifen</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No leg swelling, calf pain, or breathlessness</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Interacting medicines with tamoxifen (strong CYP2D6 inhibitors)</td><td>At each medicine change</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No interacting medicine; if a hot-flash medicine is needed, venlafaxine rather than paroxetine or fluoxetine</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Lymphedema assessment and risk-reduction counseling</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No swelling; arm circumferences equal; risk is low after sentinel node biopsy alone; counseled to report heaviness or swelling</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Distress, depression, and anxiety screen (PHQ-9 and GAD-7)</td><td>At periodic intervals</td><td>Up to date</td><td>PHQ-9 4 and GAD-7 3 three weeks ago; next at the three-month visit</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Fatigue, cognitive concerns, and sexual or vaginal symptoms</td><td>At each visit or periodically</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No fatigue or memory concerns; no vaginal dryness or pain</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Routine blood counts, chemistry, liver tests, and tumor markers (CEA, CA 15-3, CA 27.29)</td><td>Not recommended in asymptomatic stage I to III survivors</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>No symptoms to prompt testing; none ordered</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>CT, bone scan, PET, or other imaging for distant spread</td><td>Not recommended in asymptomatic survivors</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>No symptoms; not ordered</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Diagnostic mammogram of the left breast</td><td>Yearly after mastectomy</td><td>Due</td><td>In September, one year after the diagnostic mammogram</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Breast MRI</td><td>Yearly only if high-risk criteria are met (BRCA1 or BRCA2, chest radiation at 10 to 30, lifetime risk 20 percent or more)</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>No high-risk criterion known; to be revisited if germline testing finds a pathogenic variant</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Bone density (DXA)</td><td>Every 2 years on an aromatase inhibitor, on tamoxifen with ovarian suppression, or after chemotherapy-induced menopause</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Premenopausal on tamoxifen alone; bone health by diet, vitamin D, and weight-bearing exercise</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Echocardiogram (LVEF)</td><td>During and after trastuzumab; after anthracycline as indicated</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>No trastuzumab or anthracycline given</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Germline genetic testing at diagnosis, for breast cancer diagnosed at 65 or younger</td><td>Once, at diagnosis</td><td>Deferred</td><td>Not offered at diagnosis by her previous care team, a deviation from the guideline found at intake. No harm is known: the result would not have changed her surgery, which she chose, but it could change surveillance and the advice to relatives. Corrective action: genetic counseling requested today, as Dr. Lozano advised</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Genetic counseling</td><td>Once</td><td>Referred</td><td>Request sent today; on the waiting list</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Medical oncology</td><td>Shared survivorship care</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seen this morning; recommendations in the consultation note</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Healthy lifestyle counseling</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Walking about 30 minutes most days; goal of 150 minutes a week plus strength exercise twice a week; alcohol rarely; never smoker</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Continue tamoxifen daily with side effects she finds tolerable. In progress; target 2026-09-28 [S]</item><item>Complete the yearly surveillance mammogram of the left breast. In progress; target 2026-09-28 [S]</item><item>Complete germline genetic assessment, missed at diagnosis. In progress; target 2026-10-19 [S]</item><item>Keep at least 150 minutes of activity a week and a stable weight. In progress; target 2026-09-28 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: American Cancer Society/American Society of Clinical Oncology Breast Cancer Survivorship Care Guideline. CA Cancer J Clin 2016;66(1):43-73 (2016); Adjuvant Endocrine Therapy for Women With Hormone Receptor-Positive Breast Cancer: ASCO Clinical Practice Guideline Focused Update (2019); Germline Testing in Patients With Breast Cancer: ASCO-Society of Surgical Oncology Guideline. J Clin Oncol 2024 (2024); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, breast cancer monograph (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Survivorship visit in three months with PHQ-9 and GAD-7. Genetic counseling when offered. Call for abnormal bleeding, leg swelling, a new lump, bone pain, or arm swelling. Next scheduled visit 2026-06-29: three-month survivorship visit on tamoxifen. [S]</paragraph></text>

